+# lab/bio2-2026-11: more mechanisms taken from biology
+
+From 2959e45. Host: the WSL2 guest of the Ryzen host (12 threads, Go 1.24.7).
+Both arms here are models read by deterministic counts; nothing is timed.
+Each arm's rule is committed before its reading. lab/bio-2026-11's arms
+(B1 scent marks, B3 a fovea for hot entries) are not repeated.
+
+## The structure as an organism
+
+| Part | What it does | Its counterpart in nature | Where the counterpart does better |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| word0's exact last touch, expiry by arithmetic | "seen in the last W?" with no timer | a traplining hummingbird remembers when it emptied each flower and returns as it refills | — (the filter's is exact) |
+| the 7-bucket register | fine near now, coarse further back | scale-invariant time cells: fields widen with elapsed time | neurons also switch codes with rate: spike *timing* when events are few, spike *rate* when many (B4) |
+| every slot is 128 bits whatever the key did | one event costs what a thousand do | K-selection: full investment in every offspring | r-strategists and metamorphosis: a cheap larva, the adult body only for survivors (B5) |
+| cuckoo relocation | a move frees a slot along a path | hermit crabs' vacancy chains: a shell freed at one end rehouses a queue | the crabs queue beside the vacancy (the wait room is that already) |
+| the search budget's EWMA | stop searching where searches fail | the marginal value theorem: a forager's giving-up time follows the habitat's yield | — |
+| eviction of the lightest slot | a spray can't flush an offender | synaptic pruning: use it or lose it | — |
+| stash, then overflow table | an evicted key's history returns | immune memory: a second exposure meets the first's record; a seed bank | — |
+| hot entries, first come first served | wide counters for heavy keys | scramble competition for territories | contest competition: the stronger claimant takes the territory (B6, postulate) |
+| trust flags, `Upper` | fail closed | honest signals: a warning that can't be faked low | — |
+| ConstantTime's dithered pad | time says nothing of the key | protean behaviour: prey that move unpredictably | — |
+| `Resizable`: old table beside the new for a horizon | grow by replacement | a moult: the new cuticle under the old | modular growth: plants add segments (B7, postulate) |
+
+## B4 Spike timing for sparse keys
+
+**The analogy.** A neuron that fires rarely carries its message in *when*
+each spike falls; one that fires fast carries it in the *rate*, and single
+spike times stop mattering. The register is a rate code at every rate: a key
+with three events in a minute spends 48 bits on seven counters, six of them
+zero or one, and its events are known to 2.5–20 s. The bits could hold the
+times themselves.
+
+**The mechanism.** A register in *timing mode* holds, beside word0's exact
+last touch, the times of up to 5 older events as 8-bit offsets from the
+register's epoch boundary, in steps of q = unit/8 (40 of the register's 48
+bits; the pattern "bucket 0 empty, top bit set" tells it from a counting
+register and from a hot index under 2^37). A key starts in timing mode. The
+touch that would make a seventh event inside the horizon converts it to the
+counting register (exact: q divides the unit, so each stored event lands
+whole in one bucket). It returns to timing mode when its slot expires (idle
+for a horizon). `Upper` in timing mode counts an event unless it is
+certainly outside the window (its q-step ends before `now - W`); `Count`
+uses the step's midpoint.
+
+**The model** (`TestLabSpike`, `TF_LAB=1`). Per key: the real filter fed
+every admitted touch (the counting register, read when the key is in
+counting mode) and the list of quantized times (read in timing mode), with
+q = unit/10 = 250 ms at the model's 1 ms ticks. Three strict limiters on
+the same offered Poisson traffic: an exact sliding log, the filter's
+`TouchIfUnderAt` (base), and the model. 400 keys, 20 horizons. Rules read:
+`W = H = 60 s` with limits 3, 5 and 6; `W = 30 s` limit 3; `W = 10 s`
+limits 3 and 5 (these pass 6 events a horizon, so the key leaves timing
+mode: read as they fall); offered 0.8x, 1.2x and 2x the limit. And tfbench's
+velocity workload (`velWorkload`), the model's `Count` against the filter's,
+by window and by shape.
+
+**Rule (before any reading).** Worth building if:
+
+1. at `W = H`, limits 3, 5, 6, offered 2x: the model admits at least 0.95 of
+   what the exact log admits;
+2. at `W = H`, offered 0.8x: its false denies (denied while its own
+   admitted events in the window were under the limit) are at most a
+   quarter of the base's;
+3. no arm admits past a limit in any window (asserted);
+4. on the velocity workload no window's error is above the base's, and the
+   sparse shape's error at least halves.
+
+Void if the base already admits 0.90 or more at `W = H` (nothing to fix).
+A model's verdict is "worth building" at most: the two-word protocol with
+offsets anchored to the epoch, the hot index's top bit and the conversion
+under races are not read here.
+
+## B5 Larval slots
+
+**The analogy.** A frog does not build a frog for every egg. It lays cheap
+larvae, and the costly adult body is built only by those that survive
+(metamorphosis); most never need one. The filter builds the adult for every
+key: 128 bits, of which a key seen once uses word0 and the 12-bit tag. In a
+churn of mostly one-off keys, and in a spray of fresh keys (the attack of
+paper §3.3.1), most slots are such keys.
+
+**The mechanism.** A slot pair (two words) holds one adult (as today) or up
+to two *larvae*. A larva is one word: fingerprint 16, tag 12, displaced 1,
+last touch 35 bits; its register is implied (one event, at its last touch).
+A key's first touch installs a larva in a free word of its two lines. Its
+second touch within the horizon is its metamorphosis: it needs a whole
+pair, its own if the other word is free, else a free pair in either line,
+else a larva moved out of the way, else a search. A larva's 35-bit time
+wraps (9.5 h at 1 µs): a key that returns one wrap later, to a larva no
+sweep has cleared, reads Seen with probability W/2^35: high, never low.
+
+**The model** (`TestLabLarva`, `TF_LAB=1`). tfbench's churn stream
+(`workload.Churn`, capacity 200,000, the filter's own line count) through a
+placement model written for this arm: two lines per key as the filter
+computes them, the filter's search (iterative deepening, depth 3, budget
+500 with the pressure EWMA), eviction of the lightest. Two arms in one
+model: *adult* (every key takes a pair: the filter's layout) and *larval*.
+Loads 1.00, 1.15, 1.30, 1.50, 1.75, 2.00.
+
+**Rule (before any reading).**
+
+0. Validity: the adult arm's evictions at load 1.15 within 15% of the
+   filter's 4,577 and 0 at loads 1.00 and 1.10; else the model is not the
+   filter and the reading is void.
+1. At load 1.00 at least 40% of live keys are larvae (time-averaged over
+   the last horizon).
+2. The larval arm evicts nothing at load 1.30, and at 1.50 at most the
+   adult arm's count at 1.15.
+3. At load 1.00, metamorphoses that need more than their own pair's free
+   word (a move or a search) are at most 15% of second touches, and those
+   that need a search at most 3%.
+
+Worth building if 0–3 hold. Not read here: the hit path's cost of telling a
+larva from an adult, the slot protocol for a word that changes kind, the
+false-positive rate (unchanged identity bits; more entries per line), and
+the stash and register of a key evicted as a larva.
+
+## Postulates, not run
+
+**B6 Contest for hot entries (territories).** Hot entries are held by
+whoever came first (scramble): 1 entry per 128 slots, each kept by 32
+events a unit, so a squatter's 13 events/s a key fills the table and a
+5,000/s key is refused. A contest (the refused key takes the lightest of a
+few sampled holders' entries, whose history goes to the overflow table,
+flagged) fails on a fact of the register: a refused key can prove only 31
+events a unit, the squatter's own rate, so a claimant can't be ranked
+against a holder, and ranking by the time it took to fill (32 events in a
+fraction of a unit) lets a 32-event burst at each unit's start evict any
+holder. What nature adds is a neutral ground: a lek. A few entries (1 in
+16) are an arena with a tenure of one unit, counted exactly; at the unit's
+end an occupant with twice the events of the lightest of k sampled holders
+swaps with it, the others return to registers. Reading to take: squatters
+at 13–26/s on every entry, heavy keys arriving at 50–5,000/s; the share of
+heavy keys' events read exactly, and the squatter's cost to keep it under
+half, against today's 32 events a unit an entry.
+
+**B7 Growth by segments.** The line index is `(h * lines) >> 64`, which
+keeps keys in hash order: growing `lines` by a factor moves the keys of
+line l to lines [l·g, (l+1)·g), in order. A table could grow in place a
+stretch at a time, as a shoot adds internodes, instead of holding two
+tables for a horizon. `alt` is not order-preserving (`x(fp) - l mod
+lines`): it would have to become an involution inside a stretch (E49's
+chunks) for a displaced entry to be found by its key after the stretch
+grows. Reading to take: peak bytes over a 1.6x grow against `Resizable`'s
+1.6x + 1x.
+
+## B5b Larval slots, larvae spread
+
+Designed after B5's reading (clause 3 failed: 60.5% of metamorphoses at
+load 1.00 needed a move, because a new larva was put beside another larva
+whenever it could be), so this rule is written knowing it.
+
+**B5b** (`TF_LARVA_SPREAD=1`). A new larva takes a cell of an empty pair
+first, and shares a pair only when its line has no empty pair. Everything
+else as B5.
+
+**Rule.** B5's clauses 0 to 3 unchanged (validity; larvae at least 40% of
+live keys; no eviction at load 1.30 and at most 3,985, the adult arm's at
+1.15, at 1.50; at load 1.00 at most 15% of metamorphoses need a move or a
+search and at most 3% a search). Read also at load 1.15, as it falls.
+
+## Readings
+
